<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Keonthal State</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Junga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading state of the Shimla hills, part of whose land was given up to the British for the hill station of Shimla in 1830.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The ruling family claimed descent from the Sen dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>; tradition places the start of its rule around 1100. The capital was at Junga, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Keonthal was the overlord of several smaller hill chiefships, including Koti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theog">Theog</a> and Ratesh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkha</a> armies of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a> occupied it in the early 19th century. After 1815 the British restored the Raja, and in 1830 part of Keonthal's land was taken in exchange for other territory to create <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, the later summer capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Keonthal merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> in April 1948. Junga is now a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> district.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1100 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.59%;width:16.88%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl cla